First Author: Liou
Title: Survival machine learning methods for mortality prediction after heart transplantation in the contemporary era
Source number: 30  ￼

Very short summary: This study benchmarked survival machine-learning methods for predicting 1-year mortality after heart transplantation in the contemporary post-2018 allocation era. Five key findings were: (1) the post-policy cohort included 7,160 adult first-time heart-only transplant recipients from SRTR; (2) crude 1-year survival was 90.9%, with 506 deaths; (3) Random Survival Forests and Cox Gradient Boost performed best in the post-policy cohort, with holdout C-indices of 0.628 and 0.627; (4) model performance was only moderate overall, with CoxPH worst at 0.585 in the post-policy holdout; and (5) variable importance shifted between pre-policy and post-policy eras, with factors such as absence of ECMO gaining relevance post-policy. Figures 1–2 and Tables 1–2 were directly used for this extraction.

