	•	Section 2 Comments/Quotes: The post-policy cohort included “7,160 adult heart-only transplant recipients.” The average recipient age was 53.5 years, and 27.5% were female. Race was reported, with 72.1% white, and numerous clinical/history variables were included such as diabetes, prior cardiac surgery, dialysis, VAD, ECMO, IABP, bilirubin, GFR, viral serologies, and functional status.  ￼

Section 3: Intervention & AI Methods
	•	Primary ML Component: Yes
	•	Study Design: Retrospective Cohort
	•	AI Model Architecture: Random Forest
	•	Algorithm Name: Lasso, Ridge, Elastic Net, Cox Gradient Boost, XGBoost Linear, XGBoost Tree, and Random Survival Forests
	•	Input Variables (Data Modality): Tabular (EMR/Clinical data), Donor metrics
	•	Comparator / Standard of Care: Other
	•	Validation Method: Internal Split (Train/Test)
	•	Explainability / Interpretability Used: No
	•	Feature Selection Method: Manual/Clinical expertise
	•	Hyperparameter Tuning Reported: Yes
	•	Section 3 Comments/Quotes: The study benchmarked seven survival ML/statistical models plus Cox PH using SRTR tabular recipient, donor, waitlist, clinical, and procedural variables. Predictors were chosen “based on expert opinion and literature review.” The data were split 90% training and 10% holdout, with nested repeated cross-validation within the derivation set and random-search hyperparameter tuning. There was no SHAP/LIME-style explainability.  ￼

Section 4: AI Quality & Reproducibility (CONVINCE Standards)
	•	Missing Data Handling: Multiple Imputation
	•	Class Imbalance Addressed: No
	•	Code Availability: Yes
	•	Data Preprocessing / Normalization Described: Yes
	•	Training Size (N): 6444
	•	Test Size (N): 716
	•	Section 4 Comments/Quotes: The authors used “multiple imputation with chained equations,” z-score standardization, and one-hot encoding, while excluding predictors with >20% missingness. A 90/10 split implies about 6,444 training and 716 holdout patients. I found no resampling or class-weighting for imbalance. The paper explicitly states that “The code used in the analysis can be found” on GitHub.  ￼

